## 4582e356 Pick each tick's lease step from the two clocks, ahead of the keeper that will call it

The keeper's fence 4 rests on one decision each second: take a term, renew, or fence. It lives in a pure function of two
plain clocks, so that a Mac that sleeps or a Docker VM whose clock jumps can be shown in a table, with no timer and no
database. A lease ends when either clock says so, and a tick late on either clock, or a wall clock gone back more than
a second, fences a lease that still looks valid. This is the pure part of plan 3 task 4; nothing calls it yet, and the
lease store and the keeper's loop come with that task's own writer.

Tests: the plan's table, then each limit on both sides of it with the clocks apart, then the rows where two rules match
and only the order decides. Each boundary and order rule was broken by hand once, 23 edits, and a row failed every time.

Ruling: a tick both late and with the wall clock gone back names the late gap - why: the plan lists the late gap first and gives no order between the two reasons, and the step is a fence either way - cost if wrong: one log line names the other reason.
Ruling: a held lease with last null skips the tick's check and goes to leaseValid - why: the type allows it and there is no gap to measure, while the lease's own age still fails closed - cost if wrong: none the keeper can reach, since last is set from the first tick and held only after it.
Ruling: renew's kind is maintenance or normal, also for a held split lease - why: plan 3 has no split input and nothing here takes a split lease; plan 10 task 3 adds the input - cost if wrong: none before plan 10, which replaces that line.

